Z||000010||fierospeeder||02-11-2001||03:40 PM||||You can try emailing or clicking the unsubscribe links that you get with email.<P>Also you can try contacting the website that they signed you up for, if its really serious and usually the web owner, can get that persons ip number.||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||02-13-2001||12:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierospeeder:<BR><B>You can try emailing or clicking the unsubscribe links that you get with email.<P>Also you can try contacting the website that they signed you up for, if its really serious and usually the web owner, can get that persons ip number.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>anybody sending you unsolicited stuff is supposed to have an 'unsubscribe' link by law. Well ive found that 98% of those dont work, or it just comes rite back. I love the ones that give you a toll free number. I call them and when I get a live person I talk their azz off about the stupidist things I can think of, wasting as much of thier time as possible. I got a headset so i can work while i yap away. Its really sorta fun. great with telemarketers too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||mbramble||02-13-2001||09:01 AM||||Replying to spam with an "unsubscribe" request confirms that their spam has reached a good e-mail address.  That good e-mail address will definitely find it's way to other spammers.  <P>Most folks recommend that you never reply to a spam.  Just delete it, and/or set up a filter to stop it the next time, and/or report it to <A HREF="http://www.spamcop.net." TARGET=_blank>www.spamcop.net.</A> <P>The little note put at the bottom of many spams about it being in compliance with federal law is mostly just rubbish.  The bill quoted by most of them died on the vine in Congress in '98.  There are currently no federal laws specifically governing spam, though some states do have laws.  Spammers just use that quote as a way of making them look legitimate.  <P>Some links: <A HREF="http://www.cauce.org/legislation/s1618_hr3888.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cauce.org/legislation/s1618_hr3888.shtml</A>  <A HREF="http://www.suespammers.org/us/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.suespammers.org/us/</A>  <A HREF="http://spamcop.net/" TARGET=_blank>http://spamcop.net/</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tigger||02-13-2001||09:24 AM||||I only like my Spam in a can!<P><BR><LI>Debt Consolidation.<BR><LI>Are you Horny? (I get that one every Friday?)<BR><LI>Make money at home.  (I swear each time I get it, the amount you can make per week goes up a hundred dollars)<P>I've been leary of the unsubscribe addresses too.  I find it odd you have to email another email address to unsubscribe from another.<P>Also, I've been getting crap from<P>HotmailAccounts@hotmail.com<BR>YourHotmailAccounts@hotmail.com<BR>etc.<P>As if they are posing to be direct from hotmail and I'm so special to get their messages.<P>Looks as if they have a computer popping these things out because I see other email addresses in sent column that are similar to mine. Like:<P>racingdude@hotmail.com <BR>racinggal@hotmail.com<BR>etc. etc.<P>Sorry for the long post.<P>Where's my Tylenol?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 02-13-2001).]||||reg||8
Z||000014||rogergarrison||02-13-2001||12:00 PM||||didnt know that bramble. I thought I heard it WAS the law, I stand corrected. I still like making my annoying phone calls though, lol. Some of them really get pissed at me too, they figure if i stay on the line Ill end up buying whatever there selling, so they just stay with me.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mbramble||02-13-2001||01:30 PM||||Yea Roger, that is a great idea!!  Anything to cause those scumbags some aggravation is a good thing.<P>I have never, ever used the e-mail address that I received from my ISP.  I use a 'permanent' one that I forward to my ISP assigned e-mail address and I give out the permanent address.  That way, if I change ISP's I don't have to change my e-mail address, just point my permanent one to the new ISP assigned address.  However, every day I get several e-mails that tell me that I'm receiving it because I "opted-in" to some mail list.  And they are all to an e-mail address that I have never used.  Must be my ISP selling addresses!!!<P>It just gets very tiresome.  Everytime I start getting a new piece of spam that comes over and over, I add a rule to delete it without viewing it in my e-mail client.  Then, another one will come along and I have to work around it......||||reg||1
Z||000016||Oreif||02-13-2001||02:35 PM||||When a site wants my Email address I usually give them a Yahoo address. I set up an account specifically for it. The thing I like about Yahoo is you can block the address coming in. Usually I check it once a month then block all and delete. Some get tricky and change the address slightly so I block those too.<P>As for turning off cookies, right click IE icon, select properties and on IE4 and below select advanced and scroll down to cookies. IE5 requires you to go under the security tab.<P>Follow this link and you can get a program that allows you to use an annonymous cookie. You can turn it on and off as needed!<BR> <A HREF="http://www.cookiecentral.com/dsclm.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cookiecentral.com/dsclm.htm</A> <P>The site has lots of info on how they get your Email. Like when the GIF banners come up they actually grab your cookies!||||reg||1
